{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who rarely receive credit for waking it. Bakers lift silver trays from ovens, bus drivers test their brakes, nurses trade quiet reports beneath fluorescent lights. A newspaper lands on a porch with a soft slap, and somewhere a janitor turns a key in a school door. None of these acts is dramatic, yet together they form the hinge on which morning swings.\n\nI like thinking about such hinges: the unnoticed rituals that keep ordinary life from unraveling. We celebrate invention, victory, and sudden courage, but continuity has its own quiet heroism. Water arrives when a tap is opened. Elevators stop level with the floor. Someone has remembered to replace the hallway bulb before we knew it was failing.\n\nPerhaps gratitude begins not with grand declarations, but with attention. To notice a clean cup, a repaired sidewalk, or a patient voice on a difficult call is to glimpse the web of effort beneath convenience. Attention does not solve every injustice; sometimes it reveals one. Still, seeing clearly is better than moving through the world as if it assembled itself.\n\nTomorrow morning, I hope to pause before the day becomes noisy. I will listen for the first bus, watch one window brighten, and remember that civilization is not only laws, towers, or famous names. It is also countless people performing small tasks with care, often while tired, often unseen. The world turns because they show up, and its turning is a kind of shared promise.",
